:::
:::


Before the data, both models expect a share of greetings for the white names anywhere between 5% and 95% (the central 95% of the prior), which says only that a share is a share. The weakly informative prior puts 95% of its gaps between −36 and +36 points, a range that contains every gap on the curve of the case and the results of both studies, and the sceptical prior puts them between −9.9 and +9.4 points, giving a gap of more than 10 points to the disadvantage of the Hispanic names a probability of only 0.024. These are the two readers in numbers, and the check shows that neither prior puts its mass on outcomes that no experiment could produce.

## 3. The posterior of the gap {#sec-posterior}

With the likelihood switched on, the same call of `avg_comparisons()` on the fitted model returns the posterior distribution of the gap, and `marginaleffects::get_draws()` returns its 4,000 draws. Each draw is one value of the gap that is compatible with the data and the prior, and every summary of the posterior is a summary of those draws.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gap <- marginaleffects::avg_comparisons(fit_original, variables = "hisp",
                                        newdata = tibble::tibble(hisp = 0L, n = 1L))
gap_draws <- marginaleffects::get_draws(gap, shape = "long")
bayestestR::describe_posterior(100 * gap_draws$draw, ci = 0.95, ci_method = "ETI")
```
:::


Under the weakly informative prior the posterior median of the gap is −19.0 points, with a 95% credible interval from −28.3 to −9.1, and the coefficient on the log-odds scale has a median of −0.774. The reference specification of the course, the difference in proportions with HC2 errors, gives −20.0 points with a 95% confidence interval from −30.1 to −9.8. The two agree closely, because a prior this weak is outweighed by 362 replies, and the posterior sits about a point nearer zero because the prior is centred on no gap. The two intervals differ in what they allow one to say. The confidence interval is a statement about the procedure, which covers the true gap in 95% of repeated studies. The credible interval is a statement about the gap itself, given the model, the prior and the data, and the posterior answers questions that a confidence interval cannot: the probability that Hispanic names were greeted by name less often is 0.9998, and the probability that the gap exceeds 10 points in that direction is 0.960.

Every number of this kind assumes that the computation worked. Stan draws from the posterior by Markov chain Monte Carlo, and the standard checks ask whether the four chains agree (the R-hat statistic, which should be below 1.01), whether the chains carry enough independent information (the effective sample sizes, which should exceed about 400) and whether the sampler ran into regions that it could not explore (divergent transitions, which should be zero). @tbl-diagnostics gives the checks for every model of the page.


::: {#tbl-diagnostics .cell tbl-cap='Computational checks of the eight models: the largest R-hat and the smallest bulk and tail effective sample sizes of the coefficients, the divergent transitions and the deepest tree of the sampler, from 4 chains of 1,000 draws after warm-up. Source: `companion/_model-outputs/bayes_audit_diagnostics.csv`.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

