## 4. What changes with the prior {#sec-sensitivity}

Under the sceptical prior the posterior median is −11.6 points, with a 95% credible interval from −19.8 to −3.6. The doubtful reader ends up nearly certain of the direction of the gap, with a probability of 0.999 that it is negative, and much less certain of its size, giving a gap beyond 10 points a probability of 0.66. @fig-sensitivity sets the two priors and the two posteriors side by side.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![The gap between Hispanic and white names in the original audit under the two priors: the prior (dashed) and the posterior (solid) for each. The vertical line marks the reference specification of the course. Source: `companion/_model-outputs/bayes_audit_draws.csv`.](bayesian_files/figure-html/fig-sensitivity-1.png){#fig-sensitivity fig-alt='Under the weakly informative prior the posterior is a hump centred near −19 points, far from its flat prior. Under the sceptical prior the posterior lies between the narrow prior at zero and the data, centred near −12 points.' width=672}
:::
:::


Comparing two fits answers the question for two priors. The priorsense package asks it for every prior near the one that was chosen, without refitting: it reweights the draws as if the prior, or the likelihood, had been raised to a power slightly above or below 1, and measures how far the posterior moves [@KallioinenEtAl2026PriorsenseEfficientPrior]. A posterior that moves with the prior but not with the likelihood is driven by the prior, and one that moves with both shows that the prior and the data pull in different directions.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
priorsense::powerscale_sensitivity(fit_original, variable = "b_hisp")
```
:::


For the weakly informative fit the sensitivity to the prior is 0.027 and to the likelihood 0.097, against a threshold of 0.05 that the package uses by default, so the data and not the prior determine the posterior of the gap. For the sceptical fit both are above the threshold, 0.215 and 0.211, and the package reports a "potential prior-data conflict". The diagnosis is accurate. The sceptical prior expects small gaps and the data hold a large one, so the posterior is a compromise that depends on both, and someone who reports it has to defend the prior as well as the data. McElreath puts the general point plainly: "the prior is considered to be just part of the model. As such it should be chosen, evaluated, and revised just like all of the other components of the model" [@McElreath2020StatisticalRethinkingBayesian, p. 35].

## 5. The replication as new data {#sec-replication}

The replication is the external information that the sceptical prior lacked, and Bayesian estimation has two ways of using it. The first is sequential: the posterior of the original audit becomes the prior for the replication. "When you have a previous estimate to provide to the machine, that can become the prior", as McElreath describes the updating of a model, so that "each estimate becomes the prior for the next step" [@McElreath2020StatisticalRethinkingBayesian, p. 34]. The script carries the posterior of the coefficient, with its mean and standard deviation, into a normal prior for the gap of the replication, an approximation that is close for a log-odds coefficient estimated from several hundred replies. The intercept keeps the weakly informative prior, because the replication used new names in another year and the share of greetings for its white names need not match that of the original.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
carried <- posterior::summarise_draws(brms::as_draws_df(fit_original, variable = "b_hisp"), "mean", "sd")
fit_updated <- brms::brm(
  k | trials(n) ~ 0 + Intercept + hisp, family = stats::binomial(),
  data = datawizard::data_filter(counts, study == "replication"),
  prior = c(brms::prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = "b", coef = "Intercept"),
            brms::set_prior(sprintf("normal(%.4f, %.4f)", carried$mean, carried$sd), class = "b", coef = "hisp")),
  chains = 4, iter = 2000, seed = 20261105, backend = "cmdstanr"
)
```
:::


The second puts both studies in one model, with a share of greetings for the white names of each study and a single gap on the log-odds scale (`k | trials(n) ~ 0 + study + hisp`). The two routes rest on the same assumption, that the two audits measure one gap, and they give nearly the same answer. On its own the replication gives a gap of −0.1 points (95% credible interval −5.2 to +5.0). With the original posterior as its prior the gap is −4.0 points (−8.4 to +0.7), and in the joint model, expressed at the share of the original white names, −3.8 points (−8.2 to +0.7). *Regression and Other Stories* gives the arithmetic behind this agreement: in the simplest case the Bayesian estimate is "a weighted average of prior and data estimates, where each estimate gets a weight proportional to the inverse square of its standard error", which rests on "the key assumption ... that the prior and data represent two different sources of information" [@GelmanEtAl2020RegressionOtherStories, pp. 119–120]. The replication has about four times as many replies as the original, so it carries most of the weight.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![The posterior distributions of the gap from the original audit alone, from the replication alone (both with the weakly informative prior), and from the replication with the posterior of the original as its prior. Source: `companion/_model-outputs/bayes_audit_draws.csv`.](bayesian_files/figure-html/fig-update-1.png){#fig-update fig-alt='Three densities. The original audit lies around −19 points and is wide; the replication alone is a narrow peak around zero; the combined posterior lies between them, around −4 points, and is the narrowest.' width=672}
:::
:::


The combined posterior is narrower than either of the others, and it lies where neither study put its estimate, which is a reason to check the assumption before reading it. priorsense reports a "potential prior-data conflict" for the updated model (sensitivity to the prior 0.215, to the likelihood 0.261), so the posterior of the original and the data of the replication pull in different directions. A posterior predictive check of the joint model says the same in terms of the data: replicated from the fitted model, the share of greetings for the white names of the original audit has a 90% interval from 45.1% to 60.6%, and the observed share, 61.1%, lies outside it, while the observed 41.2% for the Hispanic names lies just below its interval, which starts at 41.7%. A model with one gap cannot reproduce the original audit. The failed check names the next model, one with a gap for each study (`k | trials(n) ~ 0 + study + study:hisp`), and in that model the replication gap exceeds the original gap by 19.1 points, with a 95% credible interval from 8.2 to 30.4. The two studies disagree, and the combined estimate of about four points describes neither of them. The [meta-analysis module](meta-analysis.qmd#sec-two-studies) reaches the same conclusion with the tools of meta-analysis and asks what a pooled estimate of two studies can carry.

## 6. Type S and type M errors {#sec-design}

The disagreement between the studies can have many sources, among them the names, the authorities and the six years between the audits, and one of them can be assessed with the numbers of the case alone: how a study of the size of the original behaves when the true gap is small. @GelmanCarlin2014PowerCalculationsAssessing propose a design analysis that reports, beside the power, two errors of a significant result. The type S error rate is "the probability that the replicated estimate has the incorrect sign, if it is statistically significantly different from zero", and the exaggeration ratio, or expected type M error, is "the expectation of the absolute value of the estimate divided by the effect size, if statistically significantly different from zero" (p. 643). The calculation needs the standard error of the study and a true effect, and they insist that the true effect come from outside the study: "Design calculations, whether prospective or retrospective, should be based on realistic external estimates of effect sizes" (p. 642). For the housing audit the replication supplies such an estimate, and section 5 put the gap at about four points once both studies are counted. The retrodesign package, written from the function of their article, computes the three quantities for several true gaps at the standard error of the reference comparison of the original study and at that of the replication.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The closed form of Lu et al. (2018), cited on the help page of retrodesign,
# for a normal sampling distribution; retrodesign::retrodesign() simulates a t
design <- tidyr::expand_grid(study = c("original", "replication"), true_gap = c(2, 4, 10, 20)) |>
  dplyr::mutate(se = dplyr::if_else(study == "original", ref$se_pp, repl_row$se_pp),
                result = purrr::map2(true_gap, se, \(a, s) retrodesign::retro_design_closed_form(a, s))) |>
  tidyr::unnest_wider(result)
design |> dplyr::mutate(dplyr::across(dplyr::where(is.double), \(x) signif(x, 3)))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

