With a standard error of 5.16 points, the original comparison has a power of 0.12 if the true gap is 4 points. A significant estimate would then carry the wrong sign with a probability of 0.026, and in the right direction it would exaggerate the true gap by a factor of 3.2 on average, so a significant estimate of about 13 points is what a study of this size produces when the gap is about 4. At a true gap of 2 points the exaggeration ratio is 6.1, and only at 20 points does it approach 1 (1.02). The replication, with a standard error of 2.60 points, exaggerates a true gap of 10 points by a factor of 1.02 and of 4 points by 1.7. Gelman and Carlin note that "[p]roblems with the exaggeration ratio start to arise when power is less than 0.5, and problems with the Type S error rate start to arise when power is less than 0.1" (pp. 643–644), and the original comparison crosses the first line for any true gap below about 10 points. The [experiments module](experiments.qmd#sec-power) draws the power curves of the two studies and computes the exaggeration at a true gap of 10 points, and the [simulation module](simulation.qmd#sec-power) counts the same exaggeration in simulated audits.

None of this shows that the original estimate is exaggerated. If the true gap were about 4 points, the original design would reach significance about one time in eight, its significant estimates would average about 13 points, and an estimate as large as 20 points would be rare even among them, so the filter of significance alone does not explain the published gap. What the design analysis shows is that a significant result from a study of this size says little about the size of the gap, since any gap below about 10 points that reached significance would have been reported as one of 10 points or more. The replication puts the gap of 2020 close to zero, and between the two studies the names, the authorities and the year all changed, so that the data of the case cannot say how much of the difference between them belongs to chance and how much to these changes.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Try it

Change the sceptical prior to normal(0, 0.5), the prior that McElreath uses for treatment effects in a logistic regression (p. 329), and work out what it implies for the gap in points: simulate 4,000 draws with `stats::rnorm()` for the intercept and the gap, convert them with `stats::plogis()`, and take the difference. Then run `retrodesign::retro_design_closed_form()` at the standard error of the original comparison for the posterior median of the joint model, 3.8 points, and write two sentences on what the result says about the published gap.
:::

## Further reading {#sec-further}

For learning Bayesian estimation from the start, *Statistical Rethinking* builds it from counting and simulation, with priors chosen by prior predictive simulation throughout [@McElreath2020StatisticalRethinkingBayesian], and *Regression and Other Stories* treats regression with the same habits and with weakly informative priors as the default [@GelmanEtAl2020RegressionOtherStories]. *Bayesian Data Analysis* is the reference text, including the hierarchical models that combine several studies [@GelmanEtAl2014BayesianDataAnalysis], and the *Bayesian Workflow* book sets out the cycle of building, checking and revising a model that this page follows in miniature, from the prior predictive check to the posterior predictive check that names the next model [@GelmanEtAl2026BayesianWorkflow]. The design analysis of section 6 comes from @GelmanCarlin2014PowerCalculationsAssessing. The models are fitted with brms [@Burkner2017BrmsPackageBayesian], the gaps in points come from marginaleffects [@Arel-BundockEtAl2024HowInterpretStatistical], and the sensitivity checks from priorsense [@KallioinenEtAl2026PriorsenseEfficientPrior]. The script `companion/_model-outputs/bayes_audit.R` fits every model on the page, and its outputs are listed in `companion/_model-outputs/manifest.txt`.

